    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.2151/sola.2022-016",
    "abstract": "The technique for composing a small subset of global climate models is critical to provide climate scenarios for impact and adaptation studies of regional climate changes. A recent study developed a novel statistical method for selecting a mini-ensemble of five climate models from the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 for widely capturing different future projections of Japanese climate across eight atmospheric variables at the surface. However, it remains unclear which mini-ensemble model contributes to efficiently covering the full projection ranges. Here, we rank each mini-ensemble projection around Japan among a full ensemble, showing that the selected five models capture the full ranges without systematic biases, except for relative humidity. Furthermore, we find that the widespread global warming level contributes to covering well the projection uncertainties in the daily-mean, maximum and minimum air temperatures and downward longwave radiation but not in precipitation, solar radiation, relative humidity, or wind speed. As the last four variables are sensitive to various factors, such as large-scale circulation and aerosol-forcing changes, rather than global-mean temperature changes, the model selection method featured here is preferable for capturing the wide future projection ranges in Japan.",
    "field": "Regional climate projections",
    "problem": "Although a small subset of climate models has been selected for impact studies in Japan, it remains unclear how well individual models in the subset capture the full range of future projections and what factors contribute to the selection method's effectiveness.",
    "method": "The paper assesses a statistical method for selecting a regionally-optimized mini-ensemble of five climate models from the full CMIP6 ensemble to widely capture the projection uncertainty of future Japanese climate across eight key atmospheric surface variables for use in impact and adaptation studies.",
    "task": "The task is to assess how efficiently a small subset of five CMIP6 climate models captures the full range of future climate projection uncertainties across multiple atmospheric variables over Japan for impact and adaptation studies.",
